<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.10.2: Working with Quadratic Factored Form with Leading Coefficient Other Than One</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02251</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.10.2: Working with Quadratic Factored Form with Leading Coefficient Other Than One</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c6bb8857-f053-431d-ae57-759456f9659a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Each row in the given tables contains a pair of equivalent expressions. Complete the tables by converting the given expression into <term class="glossary-tooltip">standard form</term>. If you get stuck, try drawing a diagram.</para>
<para>[Remember to use the ^ key to enter exponents. You can find this symbol by pushing shift and the number 6 on your keyboard at the same time.]</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <newline/><table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis> </entry></row>
      <row>
          <entry><para>\((3x + 1)(x + 4)\)</para></entry>
          <entry> </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the standard form of the equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(3x^2+13x +4\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <newline/><table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis> </entry></row>
      <row>
          <entry><para>\((3x + 2)(x + 2)\)</para></entry>
          <entry> </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the standard form of the equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(3x^2 + 8x + 4\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <newline/><table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis> </entry></row>
      <row>
          <entry><para>\((3x + 4)(x + 1)\)</para></entry>
          <entry> </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the standard form of the equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(3x^2 + 7x + 4\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>To find the <term class="glossary-tooltip">factored form</term> of a <term class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic expression</term> with a leading <term class="glossary-tooltip">coefficient</term> other than 1, answer parts a–d.</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis> </entry></row>
  <row>
      <entry> </entry>
      <entry> \(5x^2+21x +4\) </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>This quadratic expression has a leading coefficient of 5. Identify the factors of 5 to determine the leading term of each binomial in factored form.</para>
    <para>\(({\style{color:red}\_}{\style{color:red}\_}{\style{color:red}\_}\;x+\_\_\_)({\style{color:red}\_}{\style{color:red}\_}{\style{color:red}\_}\;x+\_\_\_)\)</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the factors of 5.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>5 and 1; the factored form will have the structure \((5x +\_\_\_)(x + \_\_\_)\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>The constant term in this quadratic expression is +4. Identify all the factors of +4.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the factors of +4.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>1 and 4
      2 and 2</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>The <term class="glossary-tooltip">linear term</term> in the expression is \(21x\). Place each of the factors of +4 you found in part b into the remaining blanks of the factored form structure from part a. Which of these results in a linear term of \(21x\) when you use FOIL? (Keep in mind that the order you place these factors into your factored form structure might affect the outcome, so try both orders if needed.)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>1 and 4; 1 must be placed in the binomial with \(5x\). 4 must be placed in the binomial with \(x\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the factored form of \(5x^2+ 21x + 4\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the factored form of \(5x^2+ 21x + 4\).</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((5x + 1)(x + 4)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Use the strategy you developed in question 4 to find the factored form of the quadratic expression shown here in standard form.</para>
    <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis> </entry></row>
      <row>
          <entry> </entry>
          <entry> \(3x^2 + 7x + 4\) </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the factored form of \(3x^2 + 7x + 4\).</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((3x + 4)(x + 1)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
<exercise>
  <problem> 
     
     
   
   
   
  </problem></exercise>
<!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para>Find the factored form of the quadratic expression shown.</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis> </entry></row>
      <row>
          <entry> </entry>
          <entry><para>\(6x^2+ 11x − 10\)</para></entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
    
    <para>This quadratic expression is different from those in the activity, since its leading coefficient has more than one set of possible factors.</para>
    
    <para>Use the strategy you learned in the activity to factor the expression. If the pair of factors you selected using the leading coefficient, 6, do not give you the correct solution, begin again with another set of factors.</para>
    
    <para>The expression is also different since the constant term is negative, -10. This means when you consider the factors of the constant term, one must be positive and one must be negative.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the factored form of \(6x^2+ 11x − 10\).</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((3x − 2)(2x + 5)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
</section>

<note class="self-check">
  <para>Find the factored form of the quadratic expression \(3x^2+ 5x − 2\).</para>
</note>

<section><title>Working with Expressions of the Form \((px + m)(qx + n)\)</title>
  <para>You have factored and solved many different types of <term class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic equations</term>, but the leading <term class="glossary-tooltip">coefficient</term> of the \(x^2\) term was often 1.</para>
  <para>Let’s look at the <term class="glossary-tooltip">factored form</term> of a more complex expression to better understand the process.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
  <para>Write \((4x + 1)(x + 3)\) in <term class="glossary-tooltip">standard form</term>.</para>
  <para>To find the <term class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic expression</term> in standard form, use the distributive property of multiplication over addition, or FOIL.</para>
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\left(4x+1\right)\left(x+3\right)&amp;=&amp; 4x^2+12x+x+3\\&amp;=&amp; 4x^2+13x+3\end{array}\)</para>
  <para>Now, let’s try to go the other direction from standard form to factored form.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
  <para>Write \(2x^2+ 13x + 15\) in factored form.</para>
  <para>Since the leading coefficient is 2, we know the factored form must have the structure:</para>
  <para>\((2x +\_\_\_)(x + \_\_\_)\)</para>
  <para>Since the constant term is +15, the two missing numbers in the factored form must have a product of 15.</para>
  <para>The possibilities include:</para>
  <para>+1 and +15
    +3 and +5
    -1 and -15
    -3 and -5</para>
  <para>Since the middle term, \(13x\), is positive, we know that only the positive factors need to be considered.</para>
  <para>Since the two factors are already different inside the parentheses, the order in which we place the numbers is important. We will try both, if necessary.</para>
  <para>Let’s try \({\style{color:red}1}\) and \({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}\).</para>
  <para>\(\left(2x+{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}\right)\left(x+{\style{color:red}1}\right)\)
    \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+2x+15x+15\\&amp;=&amp;2x^2+17x+15\end{array}\) not equivalent to the original expression</para>
  <para>Try reordering the same numbers.</para>
  <para>\(\left(2x+{\style{color:red}1}\right)\left(x+{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}\right)\)\(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+30x+x+15\\&amp;=&amp;2x^2+31x+15\end{array}\) not equivalent to the original expression</para>
  <para>Let’s try \({\style{color:blue}3}\) and \({\style{color:blue}5}\).</para>
  <para>\(\left(2x+{\style{color:blue}5}\right)\left(x+{\style{color:blue}3}\right)\)
    \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+6x+5x+15\\&amp;=&amp;2x^2+11x+15\end{array}\)
    not equivalent to the original expression</para>
  <para>Try reordering the same numbers.</para>
  <para>\(\left(2x + {\style{color:blue}3}\right)\left(x + {\style{color:blue}5}\right)\)
    \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+ 10x + 3x + 15 \\
    &amp;=&amp;2x^2+ 13x + 15\end{array}\)
    \({\style{color:red}\checkmark}\) equivalent to the original expression</para>
  <para>So, the factored form of \(2x^2 + 13x + 15\) is \((2x + 3)(x + 5)\).</para>
  <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Working with Expressions of the Form \((px + m)(qx + n)\)</title>
  <exercise>
    <problem>
      
      <para>Find the factored form of \(3x^2− 11x − 4\).</para>
    
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
    
    </problem><solution>
    <para>Here is how to find the factored form:</para>
    
    <para>The factored form will have the structure: \((3x +\_\_\_)(x +\_\_\_)\).</para>
    
    <para>The factors of -4 are:</para>
    
    <para>-4 and 1
      4 and -1
      -2 and 2</para>
    
    <para>Try -4 and 1.</para>
    
    <para>\(\left(3x-4\right)\left(x+1\right)\)
      \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-4x+3x-4\\&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-x-4\end{array}\)
      not equivalent to the original expression</para>
    
    <para>Try reordering.</para>
    
    <para>\(\left(3x+1\right)\left(x-4\right)\)
      \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-12x+x-4\\&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-11x-4\end{array}\)
      \({\style{color:red}\checkmark}\)equivalent to the original expression</para>
    
    <para>The factored form of \(3x^2− 11x − 4\) is \((3x + 1)(x − 4)\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
  </section>
</content>
